BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 618.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 608.0 Td (  In ancient times, two villages stood on opposite banks of the magical River of Plenty. The riv) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 598.0 Td (    er had a wondrous property: any gift cast into its waters would emerge on the far shore trip) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 588.0 Td (    led in abundance.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 578.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 568.0 Td (  The Elder of the First Village possessed five loaves of bread each moon. She could choose to k) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 558.0 Td (    eep all five, or cast some into the river for the Second Village. Whatever arrived-fifteen t) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 548.0 Td (    imes what she sent-belonged to the Second Village's Elder, who then decided what to send bac) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 538.0 Td (    k across the waters.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 528.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 518.0 Td (  At first, fear ruled both shores. "What if I give and receive nothing?" worried the First Elde) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 508.0 Td (    r. "What if they expect too much in return?" fretted the Second Elder.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 498.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 488.0 Td (  But one wise woman said: "The river multiplies gifts, not because it demands perfection, but b) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 478.0 Td (    ecause it rewards the courage to begin. Send with open hands, return with grateful hearts, a) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 468.0 Td (    nd both villages shall flourish.") Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 458.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 448.0 Td (  When both Elders embraced this wisdom-giving generously and returning fairly-the River of Plen) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 438.0 Td (    ty flowed abundantly. The villages learned that trust, once begun, creates more than either ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 428.0 Td (    could hoard alone.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 418.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
