BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 448.0 Td (      # The Myth of the Flowing River) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 438.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 428.0 Td (      In ancient times, two villages stood on opposite banks of a magical river. The Elder of th) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 418.0 Td (        e First Village discovered that any gift cast into the waters would multiply threefold b) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 408.0 Td (        efore reaching the other side.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 398.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 388.0 Td (      One day, the Elder threw a single loaf of bread into the current. It became three loaves w) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 378.0 Td (        hen it reached the Second Village. The people there, grateful and wise, sent back two lo) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 368.0 Td (        aves. The Elder kept what remained, and both villages had more than before.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 358.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 348.0 Td (      Inspired, they continued this sacred exchange. Each time the First Village gave freely, th) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 338.0 Td (        e river blessed their offering. Each time the Second Village returned generously, prospe) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 328.0 Td (        rity flowed both ways.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 318.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 308.0 Td (      But one season, drought struck the Second Village. Though they received three loaves, they) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 298.0 Td (         kept all for themselves. The Elder, understanding hardship, tried again. Yet when the S) Tj ET
